\p{The eight slowest published arena tests stress the reference engine on one thread. They were measured with the variant that has the young generation at 1 \% of the heap, two runs each: 0.83 (gf3-matrix-product) to 1.03 (magma-list-deep-n36) times the wall clock, 0.98 in the geometric mean, and up to 45 \% more resident memory on the tests that fill the old generation (magma-list-deep-n36 3.8 against 5.3 GB). With the policy as it is baked in, perf/app-lam ran in 11.2 s with 4.8 GB in the arena-shaped container, against 11.1 s with 4.0 GB before. All of them are far from the arena's limits.}

\ul{
\li{With the machine and the candidate set the two changes together are worth 5 \% in the geometric mean of the eight corpora, and most on the middle ones: con-leche 0.81, navier-stokes-euler 0.86, cedar 0.92, cslib 0.94 times the wall clock. Init and Std gain 1 to 2 \%. The runs of reachability, 0.6 s each, scatter by 10 \% under either setting, and the figure of a window has gone both ways.}
\li{\strong{Mathlib is not settled on the final image.} The one pair of runs there, 20 minutes apart and so not comparable to within 5 \%, gave 16.2 s before and 16.9 s now. In the screening, on the image and the machine jar of an hour earlier, four alternating pairs gave 17.9 s with the default policy and 17.3 s with \code{BySpaceAndTime}, and one pair 18.9 s with two scanner threads and 17.4 s with one. Mathlib has to be taken again, as three alternating pairs, on the jars that ship.}
\li{With the reference engine, the default engine at the time, the same settings gain 6 to 8 \% on Init, Std and cedar and nothing on reachability.}
\li{The price is resident memory where the old generation holds garbage longer: up to 37 \% more (the reference engine on reachability, 2.3 against 3.1 GB), Mathlib with the machine 4.1 against 5.6 GB; half of the machine's rows are within 5 \% of before.}
}
}

