\ul{
\li{With 8 workers on 8 cores a second scanner thread takes a core from the checking: one scanner thread is 13 \% faster than two on navier-stokes-euler, 4 \% on cedar and level on Init and Std; on cslib the figure of two is the median of two unequal windows, and in the cleaner one all counts are within 1 \%. On Mathlib one run each gave 17.4 s with one and 18.9 s with two. None at all is 4 \% slower than one on Init and level elsewhere; three are no better than one. Two had been chosen for the load alone, with the reference engine checking.}
\li{The automatic worker count stays at one per CPU with a cap of 8, the count found on 8 cores.}
}
}

\p{\strong{Consequences.}}
\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|rlvm FILE~} is the measured configuration; \code{\verb~|-XX:InitialCollectionPolicy=Adaptive2~} and \code{\verb~|-X scan-threads=2~} on its command line are the settings of before.}
\li{The optional build with Oracle GraalVM is built with the same settings.}
\li{The JVM launcher of a development distribution keeps its own heap flags; only the scanner-thread default reaches it.}
}

\p{\strong{Not measured.} x86-64, the architecture of the arena's runner. More than 8 cores: the cap of the automatic worker count is the one found on 8. A machine with less memory than the corpora need. The settings together with the switch set the integrator of the machine finally publishes, if it differs from the one used here; con-leche with that set is dominated by one unit under eager reduction (finding INT7-F1) and was left out of the choice.}

\p{Source: \src{scripts/build.sh} (\code{\verb~|IMAGE_HEAP~}), \src{rlvm-main/README.md}, \src{docs/research/profiles/P10.md} for the profile of the Mathlib pass; the tables of the default configuration are regenerated by \src{bench/track/benchmarks.py}.}
